1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,362
Williams Obstetrics.

2
00:00:02,312 --> 00:00:05,687
Chapter 27: Vaginal Delivery.

3
00:00:05,687 --> 00:00:25,125
Delivery Technique Persistent Occiput Posterior Position Shoulder Dystocia Special Populations Third Stage Of Labor Lower Genital Tract Lacerations Laceration And Episiotomy Repairs References The natural culmination of second-stage labor is controlled vaginal delivery of a healthy neonate with minimal trauma to the mother.

4
00:00:25,125 --> 00:00:31,975
Although some clinical settings favor cesarean delivery, for most fetuses, vaginal birth is preferred.

5
00:00:31,975 --> 00:00:44,787
For the mother, spontaneous vaginal vertex delivery poses the lowest risk of most maternal comorbidity, and comparisons with cesarean delivery are found in Chapter 30 (p. 548).

6
00:00:44,787 --> 00:00:56,275
Delivery is usually spontaneous, although some maternal or fetal complications may warrant operative vaginal delivery, described in Chapter 29 (p. 533).

7
00:00:56,275 --> 00:01:04,812
Last, a malpresenting fetus or multifetal gestation in many cases may be delivered vaginally but requires special techniques.

8
00:01:04,812 --> 00:01:08,725
These are described in Chapters 28 and 48.

9
00:01:08,725 --> 00:01:10,837
Delivery Technique.

10
00:01:10,837 --> 00:01:13,187
# Preparation.

11
00:01:13,187 --> 00:01:21,375
The end of second-stage labor is heralded as the perineum begins to bulge, and the fetal scalp is seen through the separating labia.

12
00:01:21,375 --> 00:01:26,237
Perineal pressure from the fetal head creates reflexive pushing efforts.

13
00:01:26,237 --> 00:01:32,150
At this time, additional staff to attend the neonate and instruments are readied for delivery.

14
00:01:32,150 --> 00:01:35,237
Fetal heart rate monitoring continues.

15
00:01:35,237 --> 00:01:42,712
As one example, a nuchal cord often tightens with fetal descent and may lead to deepening variable decelerations.

16
00:01:42,712 --> 00:01:48,200
If the bladder is distended, catheterization can provide added pelvic space.

17
00:01:48,200 --> 00:01:52,450
During second-stage labor, pushing positions may vary.

18
00:01:52,450 --> 00:01:58,875
But for delivery, the dorsal lithotomy position is most common and often the most satisfactory.

19
00:01:58,875 --> 00:02:02,662
Leg holders or stirrups are often used to assist.

20
00:02:02,662 --> 00:02:10,625
With or without their use, perineal laceration rates were equal in one randomized study of 214 parturients .

21
00:02:10,625 --> 00:02:16,412
With positioning, legs are not separated too widely or placed one higher than the other.

22
00:02:16,412 --> 00:02:19,475
Legs are not strapped into the stirrups.

23
00:02:19,475 --> 00:02:25,600
This permits quick flexion of the thighs backward onto the abdomen should shoulder dystocia develop.

24
00:02:25,600 --> 00:02:34,462
Legs may cramp during second-stage pushing, and cramping can be relieved by creating gentle muscle stretch, by brief massage, or both.

25
00:02:34,462 --> 00:02:39,150
Preparation for delivery includes vulvar and perineal cleansing.

26
00:02:39,150 --> 00:02:45,750
If desired, sterile drapes are placed to cover the legs and abdomen and expose only the perineum.

27
00:02:45,750 --> 00:02:54,125
Scrubbing, gowning, gloving, and donning protective mask and eyewear protect both the gravida and accoucheur from infectious agents.

28
00:02:54,125 --> 00:02:56,700
# Delivery of the Head.

29
00:02:56,700 --> 00:03:01,975
By the end of second-stage labor, the position of the occiput is usually known.

30
00:03:01,975 --> 00:03:08,937
In some cases, however, molding and caput formation may have precluded early accurate identification.

31
00:03:08,937 --> 00:03:16,200
At this time, careful assessment is again performed as described in Chapter 22 (p. 429).

32
00:03:16,200 --> 00:03:22,662
In most cases, position is occiput anterior (OA) or is rotated slightly oblique.

33
00:03:22,662 --> 00:03:28,787
But, in perhaps 5 percent, an occiput posterior (OP) position persists.

34
00:03:28,787 --> 00:03:38,325
With each contraction, the vulvovaginal opening is dilated by the fetal head to gradually form an ovoid and finally, an almost circular opening.

35
00:03:38,325 --> 00:03:43,850
This encirclement of the largest head diameter by the vulvar ring is termed crowning.

36
00:03:43,850 --> 00:03:50,150
The anus becomes greatly stretched, and the anterior wall of the rectum is easily seen through it.

37
00:03:50,150 --> 00:03:54,075
The perineum thins and may spontaneously lacerate.

38
00:03:54,075 --> 00:04:05,550
Third- and fourth- degree perineal lacerations, which by definition involve the anal sphincter and perineal body, are collectively termed obstetric anal sphincter injuries (OASIS).

39
00:04:05,550 --> 00:04:13,012
Because of their associated morbidity (p. 508), one main goal during delivery is OASIS prevention.

40
00:04:13,012 --> 00:04:23,787
To improve perineal elasticity and protect against laceration, some perform daily antepartum perineal massage starting at 34 to 35 weeks’ gestation.

41
00:04:23,787 --> 00:04:37,225
With a lubricant, the woman or her partner inserts one or two fingers 3 cm into the vagina and applies pressure, first downward for 2 minutes and then laterally to each side of the vaginal entrance for 2 minutes.

42
00:04:37,225 --> 00:04:44,150
In one large randomized study, nulliparas benefited mainly by lower rates of episiotomy and of

43
00:04:44,308 --> 00:04:46,745
first-degree laceration .

44
00:04:46,695 --> 00:04:50,495
Third- and fourth-degree laceration rates are not lowered .

45
00:04:50,495 --> 00:04:57,858
As another tool, antepartum use of the Epi-No intravaginal pump balloon similarly aims to stretch the perineum.

46
00:04:57,858 --> 00:05:09,670
However, in randomized trials, it did not prevent episiotomy, perineal trauma, or levator injury (Brito, 2015; Kamisan Atan, 2016).

47
00:05:09,670 --> 00:05:16,908
Instead of antepartum use, perineal massage can be performed solely intrapartum during second-stage labor.

48
00:05:16,908 --> 00:05:25,720
In a large randomized study, the rates of episiotomy, OASIS, and intact perineum were similar to those in a nonmassage group .

49
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:38,545
In subsequent systematic reviews, this practice was noted to lower OASIS rates and raise rates of delivery over an intact perineum (Aasheim, 2017; Aquino, 2020a).

50
00:05:38,545 --> 00:05:51,408
When the head distends the vulva and perineum enough to open the vaginal introitus to a diameter of 5 cm or more, slow delivery of the head and control of accelerative forces may minimize lacerations .

51
00:05:51,408 --> 00:05:54,533
One of two approaches may be instituted.

52
00:05:54,533 --> 00:06:01,583
With the hands-on method, the thumb and remaining fingers form a V pressed against the perineum to bolster it.

53
00:06:01,583 --> 00:06:12,770
The other hand maintains fetal neck flexion to deliver the smallest head diameter through the introitus and provides gentle pressure against rapid forward movement to avoid expulsive delivery.

54
00:06:12,770 --> 00:06:21,720
Instead, with the handspoised method, hands touch neither the head or perineum but are applied only selectively to slow rapid head expulsion.

55
00:06:21,720 --> 00:06:30,495
In a large randomized trial with more than 5000 gravidas, rates of intact perineum or OASIS did not differ between groups.

56
00:06:30,495 --> 00:06:34,745
In the hands-poised cohort, episiotomy rates were lower .

57
00:06:34,745 --> 00:06:42,433
Subsequent systematic reviews of randomized trials similarly note lower episiotomy rates with a hands-poised approach.

58
00:06:42,433 --> 00:06:56,083
However, among these analyses, intact perineum or OASIS rates were not consistently benefitted by this method (Aasheim, 2017; Huang, 2020; PierceWilliams, 2019).

59
00:06:56,083 --> 00:07:05,633
Alternatively, if expulsive efforts are inadequate or an expedited delivery is needed, the Ritgen maneuver may be employed or an episiotomy cut.

60
00:07:05,633 --> 00:07:13,933
With the maneuver, gloved fingers grasp the fetal chin just behind or in the anus and exert upward pressure between contractions.

61
00:07:13,933 --> 00:07:20,183
As a counter, the other hand presses against the occiput to control forces against the perineum .

62
00:07:20,183 --> 00:07:24,783
Although expeditious, this maneuver does not alter OASIS rates .

63
00:07:24,783 --> 00:07:27,345
Figure 27-1.

64
00:07:27,345 --> 00:07:29,758
Modified Ritgen maneuver.

65
00:07:29,758 --> 00:07:38,883
Moderate upward pressure is applied to the fetal chin by the posterior hand covered with a sterile towel, while the occiput is held against the symphysis.

66
00:07:38,883 --> 00:07:41,645
# Delivery of the Shoulders.

67
00:07:41,645 --> 00:07:50,833
Following delivery of the fetal head, a finger is passed across the fetal neck to determine whether it is encircled by one or more umbilical cord loops.

68
00:07:50,833 --> 00:07:58,220
The nuchal cord incidence increases with gestational age and is found in nearly 25 percent of deliveries at term .

69
00:07:58,220 --> 00:08:03,395
If an umbilical cord coil is felt, it is slipped over the head if loose enough.

70
00:08:03,395 --> 00:08:07,658
If applied too tightly, the loop is cut between two clamps.

71
00:08:07,658 --> 00:08:17,183
Tight nuchal cords complicate approximately 6 percent of all deliveries but are not associated with worse neonatal outcomes than those without a cord loop .

72
00:08:17,183 --> 00:08:24,995
Following its delivery, the fetal head falls posteriorly, bringing the face almost into contact with the maternal anus.

73
00:08:24,995 --> 00:08:31,583
The occiput promptly turns toward one of the maternal thighs, and the head assumes a transverse position.

74
00:08:31,583 --> 00:08:42,095
This external rotation indicates that the bisacromial diameter, which is the distance between the shoulders, has rotated into the anteroposterior diameter of the pelvis.

75
00:08:42,095 --> 00:08:49,020
Most often, the shoulders appear at the vulva just after external rotation and are born spontaneously.

76
00:08:49,020 --> 00:08:52,795
If delayed, extraction aids controlled delivery.

77
00:08:52,795 --> 00:09:10,395
The sides of the head and neck are grasped with two hands, and if needed, gentle axial traction may be applied until the anterior shoulder appears under the pubic arch. Axial traction is aligned with the fetal spine, and lateral deviation of the neck does not exceed 25 to 45 degrees .

78
00:09:10,395 --> 00:09:13,870
Next, the posterior shoulder is delivered.

79
00:09:13,870 --> 00:09:21,120
During delivery, abrupt or powerful lateral extension of the neck is avoided to avert brachial plexus injury.

80
00:09:21,120 --> 00:09:22,445
The

81
00:09:22,612 --> 00:09:27,399
rest of the body almost always follows the shoulders without difficulty.

82
00:09:27,349 --> 00:09:35,899
With prolonged delay, however, its birth may be hastened by moderate outward traction on the head and moderate pressure on the uterine fundus.

83
00:09:35,899 --> 00:09:39,124
Hooking the fingers in the axillae is avoided.

84
00:09:39,124 --> 00:09:45,512
This can injure upper extremity nerves and produce a transient or possibly permanent paralysis.

85
00:09:45,512 --> 00:09:52,562
After the newborn, a gush of amnionic fluid that can be blood-tinged but not grossly bloody usually follows.

86
00:09:52,562 --> 00:09:59,174
Previously, immediate nasopharyngeal bulb suctioning of the newborn was routine to remove secretions.

87
00:09:59,174 --> 00:10:04,574
However, suctioning of the nasopharynx may lead to neonatal bradycardia .

88
00:10:04,574 --> 00:10:13,874
American Heart Association neonatal resuscitation recommendations eschew most suctioning immediately following birth—even with meconium present .

89
00:10:13,874 --> 00:10:23,099
Moreover, with meconium-stained fluid, routine intubation for tracheal suction is not recommended for vigorous or for nonvigorous neonates.

90
00:10:23,099 --> 00:10:31,162
Suctioning is reserved for neonates who have obvious obstruction to spontaneous breathing or who require positive-pressure ventilation .

91
00:10:31,162 --> 00:10:40,099
For suctioning, options are bulb syringe or suction catheter aspiration and may include intubation and suctioning if the airway is obstructed.

92
00:10:40,099 --> 00:10:42,487
# Cord Clamping.

93
00:10:42,487 --> 00:10:55,037
The umbilical cord is cut between two clamps placed 6 to 8 cm from the fetal abdomen, and later an umbilical cord clamp is applied 2 to 3 cm from its insertion into the fetal abdomen.

94
00:10:55,037 --> 00:11:03,337
For vigorous term neonates, umbilical cord clamping is ideally delayed 30 to 60 seconds to transfer blood to the newborn.

95
00:11:03,337 --> 00:11:08,424
Resting the newborn below the level of the uterus allows gravity to aid flow .

96
00:11:08,424 --> 00:11:18,962
As a result, higher total body iron stores and lower anemia rates for the neonate are sustained (Andersson, 2011; McDonald, 2013).

97
00:11:18,962 --> 00:11:25,387
This practice may be particularly valuable in populations in which iron deficiency is prevalent .

98
00:11:25,387 --> 00:11:34,012
In general, delayed compared with early umbilical cord clamping does not worsen Apgar scores or umbilical cord pH values.

99
00:11:34,012 --> 00:11:48,087
For the mother, postpartum hemorrhage rates and puerperal hemoglobin status do not differ between early and delayed clamping groups (Andersson, 2013; Gomersall, 2021; Nudelman, 2020).

100
00:11:48,087 --> 00:11:54,812
Of potential harms, a greater hemoglobin concentration raises risks for hyperbilirubinemia .

101
00:11:54,812 --> 00:12:05,949
Data are conflicting but suggest that neonatal phototherapy rates do not differ (Andersson, 2011; Chen, 2018; McDonald, 2013).

102
00:12:05,949 --> 00:12:16,612
That said, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020e) recommends that protocols should be in place that identify neonatal jaundice.

103
00:12:16,612 --> 00:12:22,224
Other cautions are cases requiring expedited maternal or fetal resuscitation.

104
00:12:22,224 --> 00:12:30,524
Others are those having abnormal placentation or compromised placental function, such as with abruption or fetal-growth restriction.

105
00:12:30,524 --> 00:12:37,812
Fewer data are available regarding cord “milking,” in which the operator pushes blood through the cord toward the newborn.

106
00:12:37,812 --> 00:12:45,399
This maneuver appears safe for term newborns and may be advantageous if rapid cord clamping is clinically indicated .

107
00:12:45,399 --> 00:12:51,937
For the preterm neonate, delayed cord clamping for 30 seconds or longer has several benefits.

108
00:12:51,937 --> 00:13:03,799
From systematic reviews on the topic, these include decreased rates of blood transfusion and death before hospital discharge (Fogarty, 2018; Rabe, 2019).

109
00:13:03,799 --> 00:13:13,387
However, one large randomized study did not show an advantage for its primary outcome, which was a composite of death plus major neonatal morbidity .

110
00:13:13,387 --> 00:13:25,499
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020e) recommends delayed cord clamping for vigorous term and preterm neonates not needing immediate resuscitation at birth.

111
00:13:25,499 --> 00:13:29,774
Cord milking in the preterm neonate may pose potential harm.

112
00:13:29,774 --> 00:13:44,037
Although conflicting, some data suggest higher associated rates of severe interventricular hemorrhage in those <32 weeks’ gestation (Katheria, 2019, 2020; Kumbhat, 2021).

113
00:13:44,037 --> 00:13:58,637
Because of rapid blood volume changes, organizations currently recommend against the routine use of cord milking for neonates born <29 weeks’ gestation (Seidler, 2021; Wyckoff, 2015).

114
00:13:58,637 --> 00:14:01,849
# Occiput Transverse Position.

115
00:14:01,849 --> 00:14:07,587
In some cases, pelvic shape leads to a persistent occiput transverse (OT)

116
00:14:07,756 --> 00:14:12,243
position that is not easily overcome during second-stage pushing.

117
00:14:12,193 --> 00:14:21,181
A platypelloid pelvis is flattened anteroposteriorly and an android pelvis is heart shaped (Fig. 2-16, p. 29).

118
00:14:21,181 --> 00:14:28,231
With these, space may be inadequate for occipital rotation to either an anterior or posterior position.

119
00:14:28,231 --> 00:14:35,956
In the absence of a pelvic architectural abnormality or asynclitism, the OT position is usually transitory.

120
00:14:35,956 --> 00:14:43,156
Thus, unless contractions are hypotonic, the head usually rotates spontaneously to an OA position.

121
00:14:43,156 --> 00:14:49,168
If rotation ceases because of poor expulsive forces, vaginal delivery can be aided.

122
00:14:49,168 --> 00:14:58,218
The easiest is manual rotation of the occiput either anteriorly to an OA or, less commonly, posteriorly to an OP position.

123
00:14:58,218 --> 00:15:02,668
Kielland forceps also can rotate the occiput anteriorly.

124
00:15:02,668 --> 00:15:09,093
Both manual and forceps rotations are described in Chapter 29 (p. 540).

125
00:15:09,093 --> 00:15:12,981
# Persistent Occiput Posterior Position.

126
00:15:12,981 --> 00:15:19,943
Approximately 2 to 10 percent of singleton, term, cephalic fetuses deliver in an OP position .

127
00:15:19,943 --> 00:15:25,756
Many fetuses delivering OP are OA in early labor but malrotate during labor.

128
00:15:25,756 --> 00:15:40,381
Predisposing risks include epidural analgesia, nulliparity, greater fetal weight, and prior delivery with OP positioning (Cheng, 2006a; Gardberg, 2004; Lieberman, 2005).

129
00:15:40,381 --> 00:15:50,343
Of pelvic shapes, an anthropoid pelvis and narrow subpubic angle can predispose (Barth, 2015; Ghi, 2016).

130
00:15:50,343 --> 00:15:59,981
Women with a persistent OP position have higher associated rates of prolonged second-stage labor, cesarean delivery, and operative vaginal delivery.

131
00:15:59,981 --> 00:16:07,506
For women who deliver vaginally, rates of blood loss and of OASIS are increased (Senécal, 2005).

132
00:16:07,506 --> 00:16:13,868
Newborns delivered from an OP position have higher complication rates than those born positioned OA.

133
00:16:13,868 --> 00:16:32,581
In one study of neonatal outcomes in 2591 women undergoing delivery from a persistent OP position, rates of acidemic umbilical cord gases, birth trauma, Apgar scores <7, and intensive care nursery admission, among others, were higher compared with an OA position .

134
00:16:32,581 --> 00:16:39,143
Others report similar findings (Fitzpatrick, 2001; Ponkey, 2003).

135
00:16:39,143 --> 00:16:43,031
Fetal head position assessment is essential to management .

136
00:16:43,031 --> 00:16:52,793
However, digital examination for identification of fetal head position can be inaccurate, and transabdominal sonography may increase accuracy .

137
00:16:52,793 --> 00:16:58,356
The transducer is placed transversely just cephalad to the maternal mons pubis.

138
00:16:58,356 --> 00:17:05,643
In the sonogram, fetal orbits and nasal bridge lie ventrally, whereas the occiput apposes the lower sacrum.

139
00:17:05,643 --> 00:17:12,643
Such information may explain prolonged second-stage labor and identify suitable candidates for rotation.

140
00:17:12,643 --> 00:17:25,556
Of other interventions, varying maternal position either antepartum or during labor does not appear to lower rates of persistent OP position (Kariminia, 2004; Le Ray, 2016).

141
00:17:25,556 --> 00:17:34,581
If the bony pelvic outlet is roomy and the perineum is somewhat relaxed from prior deliveries, rapid spontaneous OP delivery can take place.

142
00:17:34,581 --> 00:17:41,368
Conversely, if the perineum is resistant to stretch, second-stage labor may be appreciably prolonged.

143
00:17:41,368 --> 00:17:48,843
During each maternal push, the head is driven against the perineum to a greater degree than when the head position is OA.

144
00:17:48,843 --> 00:17:51,931
This leads to greater rates of OASIS .

145
00:17:51,931 --> 00:17:58,956
For these reasons, manual rotation with spontaneous delivery from an OA position has advantages.

146
00:17:58,956 --> 00:18:08,768
Lower rates of cesarean delivery, severe perineal laceration, chorioamnionitis, and maternal blood loss follow rotation to OA position .

147
00:18:08,768 --> 00:18:15,581
Alternatively, forceps rotation to an OA position may be attempted for those with suitable skills.

148
00:18:15,581 --> 00:18:22,181
Last, forceps or vacuum device also can be applied and delivery completed from an OP position.

149
00:18:22,181 --> 00:18:28,218
These operative vaginal techniques are detailed in Chapter 29 (p. 540).

150
00:18:28,218 --> 00:18:34,243
Infrequently, protrusion of fetal scalp through the introitus can be erroneously encouraging.

151
00:18:34,243 --> 00:18:42,581
In these cases, findings reflect marked fetal head elongation from molding plus substantive scalp edema and not fetal descent.

152
00:18:42,581 --> 00:18:51,118
In some cases, the head may not even be engaged—that is, the biparietal diameter may not have passed through the pelvic inlet.

153
00:18:51,118 --> 00:18:55,468
Labor is characteristically long and descent of the head is slow.

154
00:18:55,468 --> 00:19:01,643
Careful palpation above the symphysis may disclose the fetal head to be above the pelvic inlet.

155
00:19:01,643 --> 00:19:03,106
Prompt

156
00:19:03,268 --> 00:19:06,318
cesarean delivery is appropriate.

157
00:19:06,268 --> 00:19:08,318
Shoulder Dystocia.

158
00:19:08,318 --> 00:19:15,730
Following complete emergence of the fetal head during vaginal delivery, the remainder of the body may not rapidly follow.

159
00:19:15,730 --> 00:19:25,280
The anterior fetal shoulder can become wedged behind the symphysis pubis and fail to deliver with maternal pushing and gentle axial traction by the provider.

160
00:19:25,280 --> 00:19:31,405
One indicator may be retraction of the baby’s head against the mother’s perineum—the turtle sign.

161
00:19:31,405 --> 00:19:37,880
Because the umbilical cord is compressed within the birth canal, shoulder dystocia is an emergency.

162
00:19:37,880 --> 00:19:41,618
Several maneuvers may be performed to free the shoulder.

163
00:19:41,618 --> 00:19:47,268
This requires a team approach, and effective communication and leadership are critical.

164
00:19:47,268 --> 00:19:52,393
Consensus regarding a specific definition of shoulder dystocia is lacking.

165
00:19:52,393 --> 00:20:02,230
Some, including the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019c), diagnose it if maneuvers are required to free the shoulder.

166
00:20:02,230 --> 00:20:14,080
Spong and coworkers (1995) reported that the mean head-to-body delivery time in normal births was 24 seconds compared with 79 seconds in those with shoulder dystocia.

167
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:21,793
These investigators proposed that a head-to-body delivery time >60 seconds be used to define shoulder dystocia.

168
00:20:21,793 --> 00:20:31,380
Currently, however, the diagnosis continues to rely on the clinical perception that the normal traction needed for fetal shoulder delivery is ineffective.

169
00:20:31,380 --> 00:20:36,593
Because of these differing definitions, the incidence of shoulder dystocia varies.

170
00:20:36,593 --> 00:20:41,780
One review cites a clinically useful average of 1 percent of all deliveries .

171
00:20:41,780 --> 00:20:49,493
The incidence has risen in recent decades, likely due to increasing fetal birthweight (Øverland, 2014).

172
00:20:49,493 --> 00:20:54,743
Greater recognition and documentation also may boost reported incidences .

173
00:20:54,743 --> 00:20:58,430
# Maternal and Neonatal Consequences.

174
00:20:58,430 --> 00:21:03,793
In general, shoulder dystocia poses greater risk to the fetus than to the mother.

175
00:21:03,793 --> 00:21:15,805
The main maternal risks are serious perineal tears and postpartum hemorrhage, usually from uterine atony but also from lacerations (Hehir, 2020; Rahman, 2009).

176
00:21:15,805 --> 00:21:22,730
In contrast, significant neuromusculoskeletal injury and asphyxia are neonatal concerns.

177
00:21:22,730 --> 00:21:29,043
These specific injuries are described fully in Chapter 33 (p. 599).

178
00:21:29,043 --> 00:21:40,693
In one review of 1177 shoulder dystocia cases, brachial plexus injury was diagnosed in 11 percent and clavicular or humeral fracture in 2 percent .

179
00:21:40,693 --> 00:21:46,480
MacKenzie and associates (2007) reviewed 514 cases.

180
00:21:46,480 --> 00:21:58,943
Of the neonates, 7 percent showed evidence of acidosis at delivery, and 1.5 percent required cardiac resuscitation or developed hypoxic ischemic encephalopathy (HIE).

181
00:21:58,943 --> 00:22:09,455
In another review of 200 cases, rates of severe fetal acidosis and HIE were each 0.5 percent if delivery was completed within 5 minutes.

182
00:22:09,455 --> 00:22:17,355
These rates rose to 6 and 24 percent, respectively, with delivery delays ≥5 minutes .

183
00:22:17,355 --> 00:22:20,218
# Prediction and Prevention.

184
00:22:20,218 --> 00:22:28,493
Although several factors are clearly associated with this complication, shoulder dystocia cannot be accurately predicted or prevented .

185
00:22:28,493 --> 00:22:42,855
Associated characteristics include fetal macrosomia, maternal obesity, prolonged second-stage labor, operative vaginal delivery, and a prior shoulder dystocia (Mehta, 2004; Zhang, 2018).

186
00:22:42,855 --> 00:22:51,205
Of these, increasing birthweight creates a corresponding rise in the incidence of shoulder dystocia (Øverland, 2012).

187
00:22:51,205 --> 00:23:00,630
With fetal macrosomia, commonly associated maternal characteristics are obesity, postterm pregnancy, multiparity, and diabetes .

188
00:23:00,630 --> 00:23:07,943
The combination of fetal macrosomia and maternal diabetes mellitus escalates the frequency of shoulder dystocia .

189
00:23:07,943 --> 00:23:16,305
This predisposition may stem from anthropomorphic differences between comparable-weight fetuses of nondiabetic and diabetic mothers.

190
00:23:16,305 --> 00:23:23,918
The latter fetal group has larger shoulder and extremity circumferences and greater shoulder-to-head and chest-to-head size .

191
00:23:23,918 --> 00:23:31,993
However, translating these specific measurements into stand-alone sonographic thresholds shows poor predictive sensitivity .

192
00:23:31,993 --> 00:23:45,980
Planned cesarean delivery should be considered for the nondiabetic woman with a fetus whose estimated fetal weight is >5000 g or for the diabetic woman whose fetus is estimated to weigh >4500 g .

193
00:23:45,980 --> 00:23:50,268
As prevention, early labor induction has been evaluated.

194
00:23:50,268 --> 00:23:53,643
In one study, approximately 800

195
00:23:53,812 --> 00:24:03,387
women with suspected macrosomic fetuses were randomly assigned to early induction between 37 and 39 weeks’ gestation or to expectant care .

196
00:24:03,337 --> 00:24:07,237
Women with insulin-requiring diabetes were excluded.

197
00:24:07,237 --> 00:24:14,237
Dystocia rates were lowered by two thirds in the induction group, and neither group suffered brachial plexus injury.

198
00:24:14,237 --> 00:24:22,362
Other neonatal outcomes were similar, except for a higher rate of hyperbilirubinemia and phototherapy in the induction group.

199
00:24:22,362 --> 00:24:33,962
A systematic review of four randomized trials again with the same compared groups showed no differences in rates of cesarean delivery, shoulder dystocia, or operative vaginal delivery.

200
00:24:33,962 --> 00:24:37,599
Fetal fracture rates were lower in the induction group .

201
00:24:37,599 --> 00:24:44,812
Notably, when assessing the benefits, the poor accuracy of antepartum fetal weight prediction should be considered .

202
00:24:44,812 --> 00:24:51,537
Moreover, potential benefits of early induction are balanced against potential harms from early delivery.

203
00:24:51,537 --> 00:25:02,899
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019a,c) recommends against labor induction before 39 weeks for suspected macrosomia alone.

204
00:25:02,899 --> 00:25:09,999
Of other risks, recurrent shoulder dystocia ranges from 4 to 13 percent in the three largest series .

205
00:25:09,999 --> 00:25:15,612
For many women with prior shoulder dystocia, labor may still be a reasonable option.

206
00:25:15,612 --> 00:25:30,299
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019c) recommends that estimated fetal weight, gestational age, maternal glucose intolerance, and severity of prior neonatal injury be evaluated.

207
00:25:30,299 --> 00:25:37,787
Antepartum, risks and benefits of cesarean delivery are discussed with any woman with a history of shoulder dystocia.

208
00:25:37,787 --> 00:25:42,799
After discussion and risk assessment, either mode of delivery is appropriate.

209
00:25:42,799 --> 00:25:45,024
# Management.

210
00:25:45,024 --> 00:25:48,662
Shoulder dystocia cannot be accurately predicted.

211
00:25:48,662 --> 00:25:58,012
Thus, the labor and delivery team, which includes nurses, obstetrical providers, and anesthesia staff, should be well versed in its management.

212
00:25:58,012 --> 00:26:01,912
An emergent call for assistance should assemble these members.

213
00:26:01,912 --> 00:26:07,949
Because of ongoing cord compression with this dystocia, one goal is to reduce delivery time.

214
00:26:07,949 --> 00:26:15,149
This is balanced against the second goal, which is avoiding fetal and maternal injury from aggressive manipulations.

215
00:26:15,149 --> 00:26:18,474
Adequate analgesia assists maneuvers.

216
00:26:18,474 --> 00:26:30,824
Episiotomy may be needed to provide sufficient room for essential manipulations but itself does not lower brachial plexus injury rates (Gurewitsch, 2004; Paris, 2011).

217
00:26:30,824 --> 00:26:38,099
Various techniques can be used to free the anterior shoulder from its impacted position behind the symphysis pubis.

218
00:26:38,099 --> 00:26:41,774
The McRoberts maneuver is often selected first.

219
00:26:41,774 --> 00:26:48,899
With it, legs are lifted from stirrups, hips are sharply flexed up onto the abdomen, and knees remain flexed.

220
00:26:48,899 --> 00:26:55,224
Gherman and colleagues (2000) analyzed the McRoberts maneuver using x-ray pelvimetry.

221
00:26:55,224 --> 00:27:06,287
The procedure causes straightening of the sacrum relative to the lumbar vertebrae, rotation of the symphysis pubis toward the maternal head, and a decrease in the angle of pelvic inclination.

222
00:27:06,287 --> 00:27:13,987
This does not increase pelvic dimensions, but pelvic rotation cephalad tends to free the impacted anterior shoulder.

223
00:27:13,987 --> 00:27:24,987
Gonik and coworkers (1989) tested the McRoberts position objectively with laboratory models and found that the maneuver reduced the forces needed to free the fetal shoulder.

224
00:27:24,987 --> 00:27:31,787
During McRoberts maneuver, an assistant can apply downward and laterally directed suprapubic pressure .

225
00:27:31,787 --> 00:27:36,912
This abducts the anterior shoulder to create a shorter bisacromial diameter.

226
00:27:36,912 --> 00:27:44,849
It also rotates the shoulders into the oblique diameter, which at the pelvic inlet is longer than the anteroposterior diameter.

227
00:27:44,849 --> 00:27:50,862
If unsuccessful, most move next to the posterior shoulder or to rotation maneuvers.

228
00:27:50,862 --> 00:27:53,424
Figure 27-2.

229
00:27:53,424 --> 00:27:55,674
The McRoberts maneuver.

230
00:27:55,674 --> 00:28:02,299
The maneuver consists of removing the legs from the stirrups and sharply flexing the thighs up onto the abdomen.

231
00:28:02,299 --> 00:28:08,987
Simultaneously, an assistant also provides suprapubic pressure that is directed down and lateral.

232
00:28:08,987 --> 00:28:21,074
With delivery of the posterior shoulder, the provider inserts a hand into the posterior pelvis, carefully sweeps the posterior arm of the fetus forward across its chest, and delivers the arm .

233
00:28:21,074 --> 00:28:26,874
If possible, the operator’s fingers are aligned parallel to the long axis of the fetal

234
00:28:27,028 --> 00:28:29,903
humerus to lower fracture risks.

235
00:28:29,853 --> 00:28:35,215
With the arm now outside of the pelvis, the bisacromial diameter is diminished.

236
00:28:35,215 --> 00:28:41,353
Next, the shoulder girdle is rotated into one of the oblique diameters of the pelvic inlet.

237
00:28:41,353 --> 00:28:45,903
These steps resolve the disproportion to free the anterior shoulder.

238
00:28:45,903 --> 00:28:48,490
Figure 27-3.

239
00:28:48,490 --> 00:28:52,990
Delivery of the posterior shoulder for relief of shoulder dystocia.

240
00:28:52,990 --> 00:28:59,353
A. The operator’s hand is introduced into the vagina along the fetal posterior humerus.

241
00:28:59,353 --> 00:29:05,303
B. The arm is splinted and swept across the chest, keeping the arm flexed at the elbow.

242
00:29:05,303 --> 00:29:10,640
C. The fetal hand is grasped and the arm extended along the side of the face.

243
00:29:10,640 --> 00:29:14,103
The posterior arm is delivered from the vagina.

244
00:29:14,103 --> 00:29:22,253
Of the rotational maneuvers, the method recommended by Rubin (1964) aims to shorten the bisacromial diameter .

245
00:29:22,253 --> 00:29:31,640
A pelvic hand reaches the most easily accessible fetal shoulder, often the posterior one, which is then pushed toward the anterior surface of the chest.

246
00:29:31,640 --> 00:29:39,378
This maneuver abducts the posterior shoulder and rotates both shoulders into one of the pelvic inlet’s oblique diameters.

247
00:29:39,378 --> 00:29:45,603
Friction from the vagina also abducts the anterior shoulder to some degree during this rotation.

248
00:29:45,603 --> 00:29:50,590
Again, resolution of the disproportion can free the anterior shoulder.

249
00:29:50,590 --> 00:29:53,303
Figure 27-4.

250
00:29:53,303 --> 00:29:55,203
Rubin maneuver.

251
00:29:55,203 --> 00:29:58,703
The bisacromial diameter is aligned vertically.

252
00:29:58,703 --> 00:30:07,065
The more easily accessible fetal shoulder (the anterior is shown here) is pushed toward the anterior chest wall of the fetus (arrow).

253
00:30:07,065 --> 00:30:16,190
Most often, this results in abduction of both shoulders, which reduces the bisacromial diameter and frees the impacted anterior shoulder.

254
00:30:16,190 --> 00:30:27,290
Instead, Woods (1943) reported that by rotating the posterior shoulder progressively in a corkscrew fashion, the impacted anterior shoulder could be released.

255
00:30:27,290 --> 00:30:36,003
For performance, one hand is pressed against the anterior surface of the posterior shoulder, and pressure is directed toward the fetal back.

256
00:30:36,003 --> 00:30:44,190
The posterior shoulder is then rotated 180 degrees, and in doing so, it becomes the anterior-positioned limb.

257
00:30:44,190 --> 00:30:50,690
With rotation, this shoulder comes to lie beneath, not behind, the symphysis and can be delivered.

258
00:30:50,690 --> 00:30:55,878
In a review of these four methods, all had similar neonatal injury rates .

259
00:30:55,878 --> 00:31:02,028
Importantly, delivery duration and the number of maneuvers raises neonatal injury rates.

260
00:31:02,028 --> 00:31:08,290
If the above are initially unsuccessful, they may be repeated, and other methods may be elected.

261
00:31:08,290 --> 00:31:15,115
With an all-fours maneuver, also called the Gaskin maneuver, the parturient rolls onto her hands and knees.

262
00:31:15,115 --> 00:31:20,703
Here, axial traction against the head and neck attempts to free the posterior shoulder .

263
00:31:20,703 --> 00:31:27,540
Challenges with this include immobility from regional analgesia and time lost in patient repositioning.

264
00:31:27,540 --> 00:31:32,378
In some cases, the posterior arm is inaccessible for delivery.

265
00:31:32,378 --> 00:31:40,228
Cluver and Hofmeyr (2009) described posterior axilla sling traction to deliver the posterior arm.

266
00:31:40,228 --> 00:31:49,078
With this, suction tubing or a stiff urinary catheter is threaded under the fetal axilla, and both ends are brought together above the shoulder.

267
00:31:49,078 --> 00:31:52,065
This forms a loop beneath the axilla.

268
00:31:52,065 --> 00:31:56,740
Catheter traction then sweeps upward and outward to deliver the shoulder.

269
00:31:56,740 --> 00:31:59,128
Data continue to accrue.

270
00:31:59,128 --> 00:32:06,753
In a case series of 119 cases in which this was the first maneuver, the success rate was 96 percent .

271
00:32:06,753 --> 00:32:19,203
For refractory cases despite the above efforts, deliberate fracture of the anterior clavicle by using the thumb to press it toward and against the maternal pubic ramus can be attempted to free the shoulder impaction.

272
00:32:19,203 --> 00:32:25,115
In practice, however, deliberate fracture of a large neonate’s clavicle is difficult.

273
00:32:25,115 --> 00:32:33,490
If successful, the fracture will heal rapidly and is usually trivial compared with brachial nerve injury, asphyxia, or death.

274
00:32:33,490 --> 00:32:41,865
Symphysiotomy involves cutting of the intervening symphyseal cartilage and much of its ligamentous support to widen the symphysis pubis.

275
00:32:41,865 --> 00:32:46,628
In rare cases, it has been used successfully for shoulder dystocia .

276
00:32:46,628 --> 00:32:58,828
Using local analgesia, symphysiotomy surgically divides the intervening symphyseal cartilage and much of its ligamentous support to widen the symphysis pubis up to 2.5 cm .

277
00:32:58,828 --> 00:33:05,603
If feasible, a previously inserted urinary catheter is displaced from the midline with the index finger

278
00:33:05,764 --> 00:33:09,189
of the left hand, which is inside the vagina.

279
00:33:09,139 --> 00:33:17,764
Lack of provider training and potentially serious maternal pelvic or urinary tract injury explain its rare use in the United States.

280
00:33:17,764 --> 00:33:24,439
The Zavanelli maneuver involves replacement of the fetal head into the pelvis followed by cesarean delivery .

281
00:33:24,439 --> 00:33:31,776
Terbutaline, 0.25 mg, is given subcutaneously to produce uterine relaxation.

282
00:33:31,776 --> 00:33:40,039
The operator first turns the head to an OA or OP position, flexes it, and slowly elevates it back into the vagina.

283
00:33:40,039 --> 00:33:43,226
Cesarean delivery is then performed.

284
00:33:43,226 --> 00:33:48,701
Sandberg (1999) reviewed 103 reported cases.

285
00:33:48,701 --> 00:33:55,364
It was successful in 91 percent of cephalic cases and in all cases of breech head entrapments.

286
00:33:55,364 --> 00:34:04,264
Despite successful replacement, fetal injuries were common but may have resulted from the multiple manipulations used before the Zavanelli maneuver .

287
00:34:04,264 --> 00:34:09,726
Cleidotomy consists of cutting the clavicle with scissors or other sharp instruments.

288
00:34:09,726 --> 00:34:12,701
It is usually done for a dead fetus .

289
00:34:12,701 --> 00:34:21,726
In preparation for shoulder dystocia, clinical simulations aim to improve performance, documentation, and retention of drill steps.

290
00:34:21,726 --> 00:34:36,851
Their use has translated into improved neonatal outcome in some, but not all, investigations (Crofts, 2016; Fransen, 2017; Gurewitsch Allen, 2017; Walsh, 2011).

291
00:34:36,851 --> 00:34:43,476
At our institution, these drills involve resident, faculty, nursing, and anesthesia staff.

292
00:34:43,476 --> 00:34:45,876
Special Populations.

293
00:34:45,876 --> 00:34:48,051
# Home Birth.

294
00:34:48,051 --> 00:34:54,839
In 2017, 1.4 percent of deliveries in the United States were planned home births.

295
00:34:54,839 --> 00:34:59,076
Unplanned home births constituted 0.2 percent .

296
00:34:59,076 --> 00:35:09,651
Of unplanned home births in a 15-year epoch in Norway, 69 of 6027 or 1.1 percent resulted in fetal or neonatal death.

297
00:35:09,651 --> 00:35:15,364
This high rate was attributable to infection, prematurity, and placental abruption .

298
00:35:15,364 --> 00:35:19,639
Multiparity and distance from the hospital were ascribed risks .

299
00:35:19,639 --> 00:35:27,289
In the United States, youth, lack of prenatal care, minority race, and lower educational attainment are risks .

300
00:35:27,289 --> 00:35:43,964
With planned home birth of a low-risk pregnancy, benefits include fewer medical interventions that include labor augmentation, episiotomy, operative vaginal delivery, and cesarean delivery (Bolten, 2016; Cheyney, 2014).

301
00:35:43,964 --> 00:35:56,051
Regarding the safety of planned home birth, data from randomized trials are lacking, and large observational studies derive from heterogeneous care systems, whose results may not be generalizable.

302
00:35:56,051 --> 00:36:02,501
For example, several developed countries deliver a large volume of carefully screened women at home.

303
00:36:02,501 --> 00:36:14,276
Gravidas are attended by midwives with substantial training and in a setting closely integrated with the local health-care system (Comeau, 2018; de Jonge, 2015).

304
00:36:14,276 --> 00:36:18,876
The level of such coordination in the United States is less uniform.

305
00:36:18,876 --> 00:36:26,201
Overall, neonatal risks of home births in the United States are small but greater than those of hospital delivery.

306
00:36:26,201 --> 00:36:33,114
Midwife-attended home births carry a neonatal mortality risk of 1.3 per 1000 births.

307
00:36:33,114 --> 00:36:40,351
This is a nearly fourfold greater rate compared with midwife-attended hospital births (Grünebaum, 2020).

308
00:36:40,351 --> 00:36:49,239
The most common underlying causes of death are those attributed to labor and delivery events, to congenital anomalies, and to infection.

309
00:36:49,239 --> 00:37:04,489
Of neonatal injuries, rates of neonatal seizure and serious neurological dysfunction also are elevated in home-birth groups (Grünebaum, 2013, 2014, 2017; Snowden, 2015).

310
00:37:04,489 --> 00:37:15,201
Importantly, substantial risks attend home birth for those with preeclampsia, with multifetal gestation, with prior cesarean delivery, or with breech presentation .

311
00:37:15,201 --> 00:37:25,026
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020b) specifically considers the last three to be absolute contraindications.

312
00:37:25,026 --> 00:37:34,739
Further, the College considers accredited hospitals and birthing centers to be the safest site for birth but recognizes the autonomy of the well-counseled patient.

313
00:37:34,739 --> 00:37:37,014
# Water Birth.

314
00:37:37,014 --> 00:37:43,901
As one option for pain relief, some women choose to spend part of first-stage labor in a large water tub.

315
00:37:43,901 --> 00:37:54,664
With this practice, one Cochrane review found lower rates of regional analgesia use and no greater adverse neonatal or maternal effects compared with traditional labor .

316
00:37:54,664 --> 00:38:01,814
For second-stage labor, water birth similarly aims to relieve pain, but it can pose neonatal risks.

317
00:38:01,814 --> 00:38:03,451
The rate of

318
00:38:03,604 --> 00:38:12,104
cord avulsion during water birth approximates 3 per 1000 births and stems primary from abruptly bringing the newborn out of the water .

319
00:38:12,054 --> 00:38:17,754
Fresh-water drowning and pneumonia with sepsis are reported causes of neonatal death .

320
00:38:17,754 --> 00:38:22,541
The latter emphasizes the need for rigorous sanitizing protocols.

321
00:38:22,541 --> 00:38:39,041
Systematic reviews comment on the paucity of high-quality data but do not identify overall greater neonatal or maternal harms or benefits from water birth in low-risk, term, singleton gestations (Cluett, 2018; Taylor, 2016).

322
00:38:39,041 --> 00:38:52,766
Given the lack of robust data and potential for serious complications, the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2016) currently recommends that “birth occur on land, not in water.

323
00:38:52,766 --> 00:38:55,879
# Female Genital Mutilation.

324
00:38:55,879 --> 00:39:02,154
This practice refers to medically unnecessary vulvar and perineal surgical modification.

325
00:39:02,154 --> 00:39:17,529
In the United States, it is a federal crime to perform unnecessary genital surgery on a girl younger than 18 years and is condemned by major health organizations that include the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2019b).

326
00:39:17,529 --> 00:39:25,466
Despite this, forms of female genital mutilation are practiced in countries throughout Africa, the Middle East, and Asia .

327
00:39:25,466 --> 00:39:34,779
The World Health Organization (WHO) (2018a) classifies female genital mutilation (FGM) into four types .

328
00:39:34,779 --> 00:39:42,141
Some long-term risks include propensity for urogenital infection, chronic vulvar pain, and dyspareunia .

329
00:39:42,141 --> 00:39:49,016
Thus, early in care, new gravidas are asked about these problems or prior obstetrical complications.

330
00:39:49,016 --> 00:39:56,104
The psychiatric consequences also can be profound, and referral for psychological assessment can be offered .

331
00:39:56,104 --> 00:39:58,679
Table 27-1.

332
00:39:58,679 --> 00:40:09,904
World Health Organization Classification of Female Genital Mutilation With physical examination, a finger or cotton swab assesses elasticity and caliber of the vaginal opening.

333
00:40:09,904 --> 00:40:16,291
Parturients with types I, II, and IV typically require only routine obstetrical care.

334
00:40:16,291 --> 00:40:21,829
Those with type III FGM or with extensive scarring have higher delivery risks.

335
00:40:21,829 --> 00:40:29,679
If an obstruction to childbirth is recognized, the advantages of surgical release are ideally discussed early antenatally.

336
00:40:29,679 --> 00:40:38,579
Division of midline scar tissue to reopen the vulva is termed defibulation but also called deinfibulation or anterior episiotomy .

337
00:40:38,579 --> 00:40:44,016
FGM is associated with some adverse maternal and neonatal complications.

338
00:40:44,016 --> 00:40:49,854
Moreover, vaginal obstruction can delay diagnosis of other pregnancy conditions.

339
00:40:49,854 --> 00:40:58,666
From a prospective study, the WHO (2006) estimated that FGM raised perinatal morbidity rates by 1 to 2 percent.

340
00:40:58,666 --> 00:41:12,966
For mothers with FGM, rates for prolonged labor, perineal lacerations and episiotomy, and postpartum hemorrhage were higher than those without prior FGM (Berg, 2014a; Chibber, 2011).

341
00:41:12,966 --> 00:41:25,916
Evidence supports that gravidas with type III FGM who undergo defibulation benefit from lower rates of cesarean delivery and OASIS (Berg, 2018; Rodriguez, 2016).

342
00:41:25,916 --> 00:41:35,566
To prevent obstetrical complications, the WHO (2016) recommends defibulation, which can be completed antepartum or intrapartum .

343
00:41:35,566 --> 00:41:43,741
In our and other’s experiences, intrapartum defibulation allows successful vaginal delivery without major complications .

344
00:41:43,741 --> 00:41:46,516
Figure 27-5.

345
00:41:46,516 --> 00:41:48,604
Deinfibulation.

346
00:41:48,604 --> 00:41:56,841
Although not shown here, lidocaine is first infiltrated along the planned incision if regional analgesia is not in place already.

347
00:41:56,841 --> 00:42:07,716
To protect underlying structures, two fingers or the tips of a narrow clamp are insinuated behind the shelf created by fused labia but in front of the urethra and crowning head.

348
00:42:07,716 --> 00:42:10,779
The shelf is then incised in the midline.

349
00:42:10,779 --> 00:42:17,666
After delivery, the raw edges are sutured with rapidly absorbable material to secure hemostasis.

350
00:42:17,666 --> 00:42:23,279
Reproduced with permission from Hawkins JS: Lower genital tract procedures.

351
00:42:23,279 --> 00:42:32,591
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

352
00:42:32,591 --> 00:42:37,079
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

353
00:42:37,079 --> 00:42:46,666
In discussing defibulation, cultural sensitivity is essential, and some women may be offended by the suggestion that they have been assaulted or mutilated .

354
00:42:46,666 --> 00:42:52,866
Preoperative counseling ideally discusses expected anatomic and physiologic changes.

355
00:42:52,866 --> 00:42:57,704
Partial defibulation uncovers the vagina and urethra, and

356
00:42:57,868 --> 00:43:03,293
total defibulation extends the incision to reveal the clitoris, if present.

357
00:43:03,243 --> 00:43:11,218
Women with infibulation often have a slow urine stream, and they perceive menses and urine to originate from the same opening.

358
00:43:11,218 --> 00:43:14,168
Both will change after scar incision.

359
00:43:14,168 --> 00:43:20,768
Affected women may be unfamiliar with normal vaginal anatomy, which will be visible following surgery.

360
00:43:20,768 --> 00:43:26,443
One excellent review is provided by Abdulcadir and associates (2018).

361
00:43:26,443 --> 00:43:34,318
Wound complications such as hematoma, cellulitis, or incisional dehiscence are rare but are discussed during counseling.

362
00:43:34,318 --> 00:43:44,968
Postoperative dyspareunia is uncommon, and in most studies, female sexual functioning scores improve (Berg, 2018; Nour, 2006).

363
00:43:44,968 --> 00:43:48,543
# Prior Pelvic Reconstructive Surgery.

364
00:43:48,543 --> 00:43:56,768
These surgeries are performed with increasing frequency in reproductiveaged women, and thus pregnancy following them is not uncommon.

365
00:43:56,768 --> 00:44:02,255
Major concerns are the degradation of repair by pregnancy and by vaginal birth.

366
00:44:02,255 --> 00:44:08,818
After midurethral sling surgery and then a subsequent pregnancy, continence is preserved for most.

367
00:44:08,818 --> 00:44:13,168
Multiple pregnancies, however, may raise recurrence risk.

368
00:44:13,168 --> 00:44:24,830
Evidence does not favor a delivery route, and vaginal delivery is suitable (Adams-Piper, 2016; Cavkaytar, 2014; Dyrkorn, 2020).

369
00:44:24,830 --> 00:44:33,218
Sacral neuromodulation units are recommended to be turned off because of unknown pregnancy effects from chronic electrical stimulation.

370
00:44:33,218 --> 00:44:45,468
From small reviews, lowered device efficacy and displaced or broken device leads can follow both vaginal and cesarean delivery (Mahran, 2017; Roulette, 2018).

371
00:44:45,468 --> 00:44:48,593
Data do not proscribe vaginal birth.

372
00:44:48,593 --> 00:44:54,418
Hysteropexy aims to correct prolapse of the vaginal apex yet preserve the uterus.

373
00:44:54,418 --> 00:45:01,830
Available case series are few and small and do not allow estimates of recurrence rate or delivery route recommendations .

374
00:45:01,830 --> 00:45:05,180
Most undergo elective cesarean delivery.

375
00:45:05,180 --> 00:45:07,843
# Anomalous Fetuses.

376
00:45:07,843 --> 00:45:21,093
Rarely, delivery can be obstructed by extreme hydrocephaly, body stalk anomaly, conjoined twins, or massive fetal abdominal enlargement from a greatly distended bladder, ascites, or organomegaly.

377
00:45:21,093 --> 00:45:33,830
Specifically, with milder forms of hydrocephaly, if the biparietal diameter is <10 cm or if the head circumference is <36 cm, vaginal delivery may be permitted .

378
00:45:33,830 --> 00:45:45,680
In rare cases in which neonatal death has occurred or is certain due to associated anomalies, vaginal delivery may be reasonable, but the head or abdomen must be reduced in size for delivery.

379
00:45:45,680 --> 00:45:53,055
Removal of fluid by cephalocentesis or paracentesis with sonographic guidance can be performed intrapartum.

380
00:45:53,055 --> 00:45:58,143
As described earlier, cleidotomy can shorten the bisacromial diameter.

381
00:45:58,143 --> 00:46:06,893
For hydrocephalic fetuses that are breech, cephalocentesis can be accomplished suprapubically when the aftercoming head enters the pelvis.

382
00:46:06,893 --> 00:46:11,543
Currently, these practices are more germane in developing countries.

383
00:46:11,543 --> 00:46:13,693
Third Stage Of Labor.

384
00:46:13,693 --> 00:46:16,530
# Delivery of the Placenta.

385
00:46:16,530 --> 00:46:22,143
Third-stage labor begins immediately after fetal birth and ends with placental delivery.

386
00:46:22,143 --> 00:46:28,730
Goals include delivering an intact placenta and avoiding uterine inversion or postpartum hemorrhage.

387
00:46:28,730 --> 00:46:35,793
The latter two, described in Chapter 42 (p. 731), are intrapartum emergencies.

388
00:46:35,793 --> 00:46:41,630
Immediately after newborn birth, uterine fundal size and consistency are examined.

389
00:46:41,630 --> 00:46:48,880
If the uterus remains firm and bleeding is minimal, watchful waiting until the placenta separates is the usual practice.

390
00:46:48,880 --> 00:46:59,443
Neither massage nor downward fundal pressure is employed, but the fundus is frequently palpated to ensure that it does not become atonic and filled with blood from placental separation.

391
00:46:59,443 --> 00:47:06,343
To prevent uterine inversion, umbilical cord traction must not be used to pull the placenta from the uterus.

392
00:47:06,343 --> 00:47:20,093
Signs of separation include a sudden gush of blood into the vagina, a globular and firmer fundus, outward movement of the umbilical cord as the placenta descends into the vagina, and elevation of the uterus into the abdomen.

393
00:47:20,093 --> 00:47:25,680
With the last, the placenta, having separated, passes down into the vagina.

394
00:47:25,680 --> 00:47:29,405
Here, its bulk pushes the uterine body upward.

395
00:47:29,405 --> 00:47:40,705
These signs appear within minutes after newborn delivery, and the median time ranges from 4 to 12 minutes (Frolova, 2016; Shinar, 2016b).

396
00:47:40,705 --> 00:47:44,068
Once the placenta has detached from the uterine

397
00:47:44,236 --> 00:47:51,361
wall, the mother can bear down, and intraabdominal pressure often expels the placenta into the vagina.

398
00:47:51,311 --> 00:47:56,123
These efforts may fail or may not be possible because of analgesia.

399
00:47:56,123 --> 00:48:03,411
After ensuring that the uterus is contracted firmly, the umbilical cord is kept slightly taut but is not pulled.

400
00:48:03,411 --> 00:48:09,936
Pressure is exerted by a hand wrapped around the fundus to propel the detached placenta into the vagina .

401
00:48:09,936 --> 00:48:18,511
Concurrently, the heel of the same hand exerts pressure between the symphysis pubis and the uterine fundus and directs it toward the sacrum.

402
00:48:18,511 --> 00:48:21,161
This aims to prevent inversion.

403
00:48:21,161 --> 00:48:26,511
Once the placenta passes through the introitus, pressure on the uterus is relieved.

404
00:48:26,511 --> 00:48:29,848
The placenta is then gently lifted away.

405
00:48:29,848 --> 00:48:35,023
Care is taken to prevent placental membranes from being torn off and left behind.

406
00:48:35,023 --> 00:48:40,861
If the membranes begin to tear, they are grasped with a clamp and removed by gentle teasing .

407
00:48:40,861 --> 00:48:43,661
Figure 27-6.

408
00:48:43,661 --> 00:48:46,573
Expression of the detached placenta.

409
00:48:46,573 --> 00:48:50,986
Note that the hand is not trying to push the fundus through the birth canal!

410
00:48:50,986 --> 00:49:00,423
As the placenta leaves the uterus and enters the vagina, the uterus is pushed cephalad by the heel of the hand on the abdomen while the cord is held in position.

411
00:49:00,423 --> 00:49:04,723
The mother can aid in the delivery of the placenta by bearing down.

412
00:49:04,723 --> 00:49:11,723
As the placenta reaches the perineum, the cord is lifted, which in turn lifts the placenta out of the vagina.

413
00:49:11,723 --> 00:49:14,486
Figure 27-7.

414
00:49:14,486 --> 00:49:21,073
Membranes that were somewhat adhered to the uterine lining are separated by gentle traction with ring forceps.

415
00:49:21,073 --> 00:49:24,236
# Management of the Third Stage.

416
00:49:24,236 --> 00:49:30,186
Practices within this stage of labor may be broadly considered as either expectant or active.

417
00:49:30,186 --> 00:49:40,586
Expectant management involves waiting for placental separation signs and allowing the placenta to deliver either spontaneously or aided by nipple stimulation or gravity .

418
00:49:40,586 --> 00:49:53,323
In contrast, active management of third-stage labor includes earlier cord clamping, controlled cord traction during placental delivery, and immediate prophylactic administration of a uterotonic agent.

419
00:49:53,323 --> 00:49:57,223
The goal of this triad is to limit postpartum hemorrhage.

420
00:49:57,223 --> 00:50:01,186
However, the value of this bundled approach is questioned .

421
00:50:01,186 --> 00:50:12,898
For example and as noted previously (p. 500), delayed cord clamping does not raise postpartum hemorrhage rates, and thus early clamping is a less important component of this trio.

422
00:50:12,898 --> 00:50:17,061
Similarly, cord traction fails to prevent hemorrhage.

423
00:50:17,061 --> 00:50:26,973
Traction does lower manual placenta removal rates and shortens third-stage labor (Deneux-Tharaux, 2013; Hofmeyr, 2015).

424
00:50:26,973 --> 00:50:37,448
Following placental delivery, we support uterine massage to prompt uterine firming but recognize that evidence for its benefits to avoid postpartum hemorrhage is not strong .

425
00:50:37,448 --> 00:50:43,948
Therefore, uterotonic drugs play an essential preemptive role to decrease postpartum blood loss .

426
00:50:43,948 --> 00:50:54,998
A single agent is given either before or after placental expulsion, and timing does not affect rates of postpartum hemorrhage, placental retention, or third-stage labor length .

427
00:50:54,998 --> 00:51:06,861
Choices for hemorrhage prophylaxis include oxytocin (Pitocin), misoprostol (Cytotec), and the ergots, namely ergonovine (Ergotrate) and methylergonovine (Methergine).

428
00:51:06,861 --> 00:51:15,123
In addition, a combination agent of oxytocin plus ergonovine (Syntometrine) is used outside the United States.

429
00:51:15,123 --> 00:51:24,123
Also in other countries, carbetocin (Duratocin), a long-acting oxytocin analogue, is available and effective for hemorrhage prevention .

430
00:51:24,123 --> 00:51:33,261
Prescribing information for carbetocin cites its use for cesarean delivery, but randomized trials reflect similar use for vaginal birth.

431
00:51:33,261 --> 00:51:39,336
Of agents, the WHO (2018) recommends oxytocin for first-line use.

432
00:51:39,336 --> 00:51:46,023
High-dose Oxytocin Synthetic oxytocin is identical to that produced by the posterior pituitary.

433
00:51:46,023 --> 00:51:52,498
Its action is noted at approximately 1 minute, and it has a mean half-life of 3 to 5 minutes.

434
00:51:52,498 --> 00:52:00,586
Recommended storage temperature is ≤25C°, which may pose problems in some low-resource countries.

435
00:52:00,586 --> 00:52:07,461
When given as a bolus, oxytocin can cause profound hypotension (Svanström, 2008).

436
00:52:07,461 --> 00:52:15,098
This hemodynamic change may be dangerous to women with hemorrhage-related hypovolemia or with certain cardiac conditions.

437
00:52:15,098 --> 00:52:21,986
Thus, oxytocin should be given as a dilute solution by continuous intravenous infusion or as an

438
00:52:22,156 --> 00:52:24,568
intramuscular injection.

439
00:52:24,518 --> 00:52:33,993
Despite the routine use of oxytocin, no standard prophylactic dose has been established for its use following either vaginal or cesarean delivery.

440
00:52:33,993 --> 00:52:43,568
Water intoxication can result from the antidiuretic action of high-dose oxytocin if administered in a large volume of electrolyte-free dextrose solution .

441
00:52:43,568 --> 00:52:50,606
Our practice is to add 20 units (2 mL) of oxytocin per liter of crystalloid solution.

442
00:52:50,606 --> 00:53:03,981
This is administered after delivery of the placenta at a rate of 10 to 20 mL/min—200 to 400 mU/min—for a few minutes until the uterus remains firmly contracted and bleeding is controlled.

443
00:53:03,981 --> 00:53:11,793
The infusion rate then is reduced to 1 to 2 mL/min until the mother is ready for transfer to the postpartum unit.

444
00:53:11,793 --> 00:53:15,093
The infusion is usually then discontinued.

445
00:53:15,093 --> 00:53:21,606
For women without intravenous access, 10 units of intramuscular oxytocin are injected.

446
00:53:21,606 --> 00:53:31,543
Other Agents Ergonovine and methylergonovine have similar activity in myometrium, but only methylergonovine is currently manufactured in the United States.

447
00:53:31,543 --> 00:53:38,806
These ergot alkaloid agents lower the rates of postpartum hemorrhage and the need for additional uterotonic drugs.

448
00:53:38,806 --> 00:53:43,406
However, blood pressure elevation is a concerning side effect .

449
00:53:43,406 --> 00:53:47,756
Methylergonovine is contraindicated in hypertensive women.

450
00:53:47,756 --> 00:53:59,918
If selected, a 200-μg dose of methylergonovine is given intramuscularly or is slowly injected intravenously in a period not less than 60 seconds to avoid sudden hypertension.

451
00:53:59,918 --> 00:54:10,118
Misoprostol is a prostaglandin E1 analogue, which has proved inferior to oxytocin for postpartum hemorrhage prevention (Tunçalp, 2012).

452
00:54:10,118 --> 00:54:21,881
However, in resource-poor settings that lack oxytocin, misoprostol is suitable for hemorrhage prophylaxis and is given orally as a single 400- or 600-μg dose .

453
00:54:21,881 --> 00:54:25,143
Side effects include fever and diarrhea.

454
00:54:25,143 --> 00:54:38,181
From systematic reviews, carbetocin and oxytocin show equivalent efficacy for postpartum hemorrhage prevention after vaginal birth (van der Nelson, 2021; Widmer, 2018).

455
00:54:38,181 --> 00:54:40,906
Carbetocin is more expensive.

456
00:54:40,906 --> 00:54:48,456
A heat-stable form may have benefit in low-resource settings that may have inconsistent temperatures during supply distribution .

457
00:54:48,456 --> 00:54:56,556
Tranexamic acid (TXA) is an antifibrinolytic agent and has been evaluated to prevent postpartum hemorrhage .

458
00:54:56,556 --> 00:55:08,556
In one metaanalysis, a 1-g intravenous TXA dose following delivery in addition to oxytocin was reported to lower the hemorrhage rate from 11.4 to 8.7 percent .

459
00:55:08,556 --> 00:55:20,268
In a randomized trial of 3891 women, however, TXA plus oxytocin did not decrease the postpartum hemorrhage incidence compared with oxytocin alone .

460
00:55:20,268 --> 00:55:24,781
Currently, TXA use is not recommended prophylactically .

461
00:55:24,781 --> 00:55:34,043
To treat hemorrhage, TXA use is described in Chapters 42 (p. 736) and 44 (p. 773).

462
00:55:34,043 --> 00:55:37,256
# Manual Removal of Placenta.

463
00:55:37,256 --> 00:55:43,231
In approximately 2 percent of singleton births, the placenta may not deliver promptly .

464
00:55:43,231 --> 00:55:58,543
This may represent: (1) placenta adherens, in which uterine contractions are insufficient to detach the placenta; (2) lower uterine segment constriction and a detached but trapped placenta; or (3) placenta accreta spectrum.

465
00:55:58,543 --> 00:56:13,368
Consistent risks for retained placenta include stillbirth, prior cesarean delivery, prior retention, and preterm delivery (Belachew, 2014; Coviello, 2015; Endler, 2014).

466
00:56:13,368 --> 00:56:30,443
For the last, in one study with nearly 46,000 deliveries, analysis predicted that 90 percent of placentas would spontaneously deliver by 180 minutes for gestations at 20 weeks; 21 minutes at 30 weeks; and 14 minutes at 40 weeks .

467
00:56:30,443 --> 00:56:37,868
Postpartum hemorrhage can complicate a retained placenta, and bleeding risk accrues with the length of third-stage labor.

468
00:56:37,868 --> 00:56:49,268
In the absence of bleeding, evidence supports 15 to 20 minutes (Frolova, 2016; Shinar, 2016a; van Ast, 2019).

469
00:56:49,268 --> 00:56:53,718
The WHO (2012) cites a 60-minute threshold.

470
00:56:53,718 --> 00:57:02,493
Notably, if brisk bleeding ensues and the placenta cannot be delivered by standard technique, manual removal of the placenta is indicated .

471
00:57:02,493 --> 00:57:12,556
When performed, some administer a single dose of intravenous antibiotics, however, one systematic review of observational studies found no benefits .

472
00:57:12,556 --> 00:57:22,306
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020d) concluded that data neither support nor refute this practice, but the WHO

473
00:57:22,468 --> 00:57:26,143
2012) recommends prophylaxis.

474
00:57:26,093 --> 00:57:35,030
At our institution, we administer a single dose of cefazolin to women not already receiving antibiotics and not penicillin allergic.

475
00:57:35,030 --> 00:57:37,593
Figure 27-8.

476
00:57:37,593 --> 00:57:40,255
Manual removal of placenta.

477
00:57:40,255 --> 00:57:49,318
A. One hand grasps the fundus and the other hand is inserted into the uterine cavity and the fingers are swept from side to side as they are advanced.

478
00:57:49,318 --> 00:57:53,655
B. When the placenta detaches, it is grasped and removed.

479
00:57:53,655 --> 00:57:56,168
Immediate Postpartum Care.

480
00:57:56,168 --> 00:58:00,768
The hour immediately following delivery of the placenta is critical.

481
00:58:00,768 --> 00:58:04,168
During this time, lacerations are repaired.

482
00:58:04,168 --> 00:58:12,155
Although uterotonic agents are administered, postpartum hemorrhage as the result of uterine atony is most likely at this time.

483
00:58:12,155 --> 00:58:14,643
Hematomas may expand.

484
00:58:14,643 --> 00:58:19,868
Consequently, uterine tone and the perineum are frequently evaluated.

485
00:58:19,868 --> 00:58:27,343
The mother’s blood pressure and pulse are recorded immediately after delivery and every 15 minutes for the first 2 hours.

486
00:58:27,343 --> 00:58:33,893
Her temperature is recorded every 4 hours during the first 8 hours and then every 8 hours thereafter.

487
00:58:33,893 --> 00:58:40,455
Vital signs may be obtained more frequently in patients at higher risk of postpartum complications .

488
00:58:40,455 --> 00:58:49,980
The placenta, membranes, and umbilical cord are examined for completeness and anomalies, as described in Chapter 6 (p. 107).

489
00:58:49,980 --> 00:58:52,880
# Skin-to-skin Contact.

490
00:58:52,880 --> 00:59:07,693
This practice aims to connect the mother and newborn dyad early to promote breastfeeding (American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists, 2020a; Moore, 2016; World Health Organization, 2018b).

491
00:59:07,693 --> 00:59:11,843
Ideally, the mother lies in a semi-reclined position.

492
00:59:11,843 --> 00:59:19,005
After the newborn is dried to prevent cooling, its chest is placed against the mother’s breast with access to her nipple.

493
00:59:19,005 --> 00:59:23,243
Its legs are flexed, and the mother’s arm steadies its body.

494
00:59:23,243 --> 00:59:31,430
Covered with warm blankets and often a cap, the neonate’s head is uncovered, turned to the side, and placed in a sniffing position.

495
00:59:31,430 --> 00:59:36,768
Mouth and nares should remain unobstructed (Widström, 2019).

496
00:59:36,768 --> 00:59:46,618
The dyad is continually monitored for physiologic stability, somnolence, unobstructed breathing, and safe positioning to avoid smothering or falls .

497
00:59:46,618 --> 00:59:50,130
# Lower Genital Tract Lacerations.

498
00:59:50,130 --> 00:59:55,193
These lacerations may involve the cervix, vagina, or perineum.

499
00:59:55,193 --> 01:00:01,393
Those of the cervix and vagina are described in Chapter 42 (p. 739).

500
01:00:01,393 --> 01:00:08,618
In general, those of the perineum follow vaginal delivery, and most are first- and second-degree lacerations.

501
01:00:08,618 --> 01:00:18,105
Lacerations are classified by depth and involved anatomy, and complete definitions and visual examples are given in Figure 27-9.

502
01:00:18,105 --> 01:00:25,905
Of these, third-degree lacerations involve the anal sphincter and are subcategorized as: Figure 27-9.

503
01:00:25,905 --> 01:00:33,105
1. First-degree perineal laceration: injury to only the vaginal epithelium or perineal skin.

504
01:00:33,105 --> 01:00:45,918
2. Second-degree laceration: injury to perineum that spares the anal sphincter complex but involves the perineal muscles, which are the bulbospongiosus and superficial transverse perineal muscles.

505
01:00:45,918 --> 01:00:53,230
3a. Thirddegree laceration: <50 percent of the external anal sphincter (EAS) is torn.

506
01:00:53,230 --> 01:01:02,705
3b. Third-degree laceration: >50 percent of the EAS is torn, but the internal anal sphincter (IAS) remains intact.

507
01:01:02,705 --> 01:01:07,868
3c. Third-degree laceration: EAS and IAS are torn.

508
01:01:07,868 --> 01:01:16,655
4. Fourth-degree laceration: the perineal body, entire anal sphincter complex, and anorectal mucosa are lacerated.

509
01:01:16,655 --> 01:01:24,230
Reproduced with permission from Kenton K, Mueller M: Episiotomy and obstetric anal sphincter lacerations.

510
01:01:24,230 --> 01:01:33,543
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

511
01:01:33,543 --> 01:01:38,030
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

512
01:01:38,030 --> 01:01:52,055
3a) <50 percent external anal sphincter (EAS) tear; (3b) >50 percent EAS tear; and (3c) EAS plus internal anal sphincter (IAS) tears.

513
01:01:52,055 --> 01:02:06,530
Third- and fourth-degree perineal lacerations are considered obstetric anal sphincter injuries (OASIS), and their combined incidence is 0.1 to 5 percent (Blondel, 2016; Friedman, 2015).

514
01:02:06,530 --> 01:02:23,693
Risk factors for these more complex lacerations include nulliparity, midline episiotomy, persistent OP position, Asian race, increasing fetal birthweight, and operative vaginal delivery (Gurol-Urganci, 2013; Landy, 2011).

515
01:02:23,693 --> 01:02:28,118
Morbidity rates rise as laceration severity increases.

516
01:02:28,118 --> 01:02:30,155
Compared with simpler

517
01:02:30,316 --> 01:02:36,016
lacerations, OASIS are associated with greater blood loss and puerperal pain.

518
01:02:35,966 --> 01:02:43,903
Wound disruption and infection rates are other risks (Goldaber, 1993; Lewicky-Gaupp, 2015).

519
01:02:43,903 --> 01:02:52,391
Stock and coworkers (2013) reported that approximately 7 percent of 909 OASIS had complications.

520
01:02:52,391 --> 01:03:04,216
Long term, OASIS are linked with approximately doubled rates of anal incontinence compared with vaginal delivery without OASIS (Evers, 2012; Schei, 2019).

521
01:03:04,216 --> 01:03:13,616
Fourth-degree lacerations pose greater anal incontinence risk than third-degree ones (Gommesen, 2020; Jangö, 2018).

522
01:03:13,616 --> 01:03:22,653
Evidence implicating OASIS in subsequent long-term dyspareunia are conflicting (Mous, 2008; O’Shea, 2018).

523
01:03:22,653 --> 01:03:28,691
To ensure appropriate repair, identification and correct categorization is essential.

524
01:03:28,691 --> 01:03:34,303
Intrapartum endoanal ultrasound has been used to unmask clinically occult tears .

525
01:03:34,303 --> 01:03:44,366
Few data currently support this imaging intrapartum, and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020c) does not recommended it.

526
01:03:44,366 --> 01:03:50,128
For women with a prior OASIS, questions may arise as to subsequent pregnancy.

527
01:03:50,128 --> 01:04:03,128
In those without anal incontinence after OASIS repair, the rate of developing this incontinence is not increased by a subsequent vaginal birth (Jangö, 2016; Webb, 2017).

528
01:04:03,128 --> 01:04:06,241
Thus, a vaginal route is reasonable.

529
01:04:06,241 --> 01:04:19,116
However, women with a prior OASIS do have a higher recurrence rate with a subsequent delivery compared with multiparas without a prior OASIS (Edozien, 2014; Elfaghi, 2004).

530
01:04:19,116 --> 01:04:28,028
The risk mirrors that of primiparas in the general population and is low (Basham, 2013; Boggs, 2014).

531
01:04:28,028 --> 01:04:36,866
But, in those who experience a recurrent OASIS, the risk of anal incontinence can be substantial (Jangö, 2017).

532
01:04:36,866 --> 01:04:45,416
Fetal macrosomia and operative vaginal delivery are factors for OASIS recurrence and can influence counseling in future pregnancies.

533
01:04:45,416 --> 01:04:51,091
Specifically, patients may choose cesarean delivery to avoid repeat OASIS.

534
01:04:51,091 --> 01:05:02,291
This consideration may be most pertinent for those with prior postpartum anal incontinence, with OASIS complications that required a second repair, or with psychological trauma .

535
01:05:02,291 --> 01:05:07,578
Planned cesarean delivery is balanced against its associated operative risks .

536
01:05:07,578 --> 01:05:10,003
# Episiotomy.

537
01:05:10,003 --> 01:05:16,741
In contrast to spontaneous perineal tears, episiotomy is intended incision of the perineum.

538
01:05:16,741 --> 01:05:20,591
Its goal is enlargement of vaginal opening for birth.

539
01:05:20,591 --> 01:05:25,416
Textbooks and organizational guidelines often differ in their description.

540
01:05:25,416 --> 01:05:33,841
Kalis and colleagues (2012) presents one classification, and we agree with the need for terminology standardization.

541
01:05:33,841 --> 01:05:40,928
Midline and mediolateral episiotomies are the two main types and vary by the angle of perineal incision.

542
01:05:40,928 --> 01:05:47,366
Involved structures mirror those found with second-degree laceration, and their repairs are analogous.

543
01:05:47,366 --> 01:05:56,303
The midline episiotomy begins at the fourchette, incises the perineal body in the midline, and ends well before the EAS is reached.

544
01:05:56,303 --> 01:06:06,103
The chosen incision length varies from 2 to 3 cm depending on perineal length, degree of tissue thinning, and outlet diameter needs for delivery.

545
01:06:06,103 --> 01:06:14,928
The mediolateral episiotomy begins at the midline of the fourchette and is directed to the right or left at an angle 60 degrees off the midline .

546
01:06:14,928 --> 01:06:20,028
This angle accounts for stretch distortion of perineal anatomy during crowning.

547
01:06:20,028 --> 01:06:27,766
After delivery and distortion resolution, it ultimately yields an incision 45-degrees from the midline for suturing .

548
01:06:27,766 --> 01:06:35,591
Used less often, the lateral episiotomy begins at point 1 to 2 cm lateral from the midline of the fourchette.

549
01:06:35,591 --> 01:06:40,303
It too is angled toward either the right or the left ischial tuberosity.

550
01:06:40,303 --> 01:06:42,953
Figure 27-10.

551
01:06:42,953 --> 01:06:47,678
A mediolateral episiotomy is cut as the baby’s head crowns.

552
01:06:47,678 --> 01:06:51,503
Fingers are insinuated between the perineum and head.

553
01:06:51,503 --> 01:06:59,828
The incision begins in the midline and is directed toward the ipsilateral ischial tuberosity at an angle 60 degrees off the midline.

554
01:06:59,828 --> 01:07:07,403
Reproduced with permission from Kenton K, Mueller M: Episiotomy and obstetric anal sphincter lacerations.

555
01:07:07,403 --> 01:07:16,716
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

556
01:07:16,716 --> 01:07:21,203
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

557
01:07:21,203 --> 01:07:29,303
The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists (2020c) recommends selective use of episiotomy

558
01:07:29,452 --> 01:07:31,764
rather than routine use.

559
01:07:31,714 --> 01:07:44,214
Evidence shows lower rates of severe perineal trauma in women undergoing spontaneous birth with a restrictive use of episiotomy (Dillon, 2019; Jiang, 2017).

560
01:07:44,214 --> 01:07:51,102
One potential indication is fetal jeopardy, and the quickly enlarged vaginal opening can speed delivery.

561
01:07:51,102 --> 01:08:05,764
Others are shoulder dystocia, breech delivery, fetal macrosomia, operative vaginal delivery, persistent OP position, and other instances in which failure to perform an episiotomy will result in significant perineal rupture.

562
01:08:05,764 --> 01:08:09,789
Surgical judgment and common sense should guide selection.

563
01:08:09,789 --> 01:08:14,314
As a result of selective use, episiotomy rates have dropped.

564
01:08:14,314 --> 01:08:25,114
Oliphant and coworkers (2010) analyzed rates between 1979 and 2006 in the United States and noted a 75-percent decline.

565
01:08:25,114 --> 01:08:32,752
In the United States in 2012, episiotomy was performed in approximately 12 percent of vaginal births .

566
01:08:32,752 --> 01:08:44,877
Before episiotomy, analgesia is provided by existing labor regional analgesia, by bilateral pudendal nerve blockade, or by local infiltration of 1percent lidocaine.

567
01:08:44,877 --> 01:08:51,077
Some instead advocate 2.5-percent lidocaine-prilocaine cream (EMLA cream).

568
01:08:51,077 --> 01:08:59,264
However, for efficacy, it requires application an hour before expected delivery, which may be logistically difficult .

569
01:08:59,264 --> 01:09:06,477
If episiotomy is performed unnecessarily early, incisional bleeding may be considerable before delivery.

570
01:09:06,477 --> 01:09:11,077
If it is performed too late, lacerations will not be prevented.

571
01:09:11,077 --> 01:09:18,752
Typically, episiotomy is completed when the head is visible during a contraction to a diameter of 4 to 5 cm.

572
01:09:18,752 --> 01:09:23,277
Few data directly compare midline and mediolateral types.

573
01:09:23,277 --> 01:09:32,477
As noted, midline episiotomy has a greater likelihood of associated OASIS (Coats, 1980; de Leeuw, 2001).

574
01:09:32,477 --> 01:09:44,564
Short-term rates of pain and dyspareunia are similar or increased with mediolateral episiotomy (Fodstad, 2013, 2014; Sartore, 2004).

575
01:09:44,564 --> 01:09:50,427
Even fewer studies compare lateral episiotomy to either mediolateral or midline.

576
01:09:50,427 --> 01:09:55,877
One randomized trial compared lateral and mediolateral types in nulliparas.

577
01:09:55,877 --> 01:10:08,977
Groups did not differ in pain scores, in sexual quality of life, or in vaginal or perineal trauma, including OASIS (Karbanova, 2014a,b; Necesalova, 2016).

578
01:10:08,977 --> 01:10:15,752
The authors also reported that mediolateral episiotomies required less time and suture for the repair.

579
01:10:15,752 --> 01:10:22,877
Thus, among the three, mediolateral episiotomy may be the preferred incision to reduce OASIS rates.

580
01:10:22,877 --> 01:10:26,739
# Laceration and Episiotomy Repairs.

581
01:10:26,739 --> 01:10:31,852
Typically, perineal repairs are deferred until the placenta has been delivered.

582
01:10:31,852 --> 01:10:37,989
This policy permits undivided attention to the signs of placental separation and delivery.

583
01:10:37,989 --> 01:10:43,677
A further advantage is that repair is not interrupted or disrupted by placenta delivery.

584
01:10:43,677 --> 01:10:48,552
This is especially true if manual placenta removal must be performed.

585
01:10:48,552 --> 01:10:53,602
The major disadvantage is continuing blood loss until the repair is completed.

586
01:10:53,602 --> 01:10:58,402
Direct pressure from an applied gauze sponge will help to limit this volume.

587
01:10:58,402 --> 01:11:03,802
For suitable repair, an understanding of perineal anatomy is necessary .

588
01:11:03,802 --> 01:11:06,964
Adequate analgesia is imperative.

589
01:11:06,964 --> 01:11:13,652
Locally injected lidocaine can be used solely or as a supplement to bilateral pudendal nerve blockade.

590
01:11:13,652 --> 01:11:18,589
In those with epidural analgesia, additional doses can be given.

591
01:11:18,589 --> 01:11:24,877
Following any repair, needle and sponge counts are reconciled and recorded in the delivery note .

592
01:11:24,877 --> 01:11:32,677
First-degree lacerations do not always require repair, and sutures are placed to control bleeding or restore anatomy.

593
01:11:32,677 --> 01:11:35,902
Here, few data guide suture selection.

594
01:11:35,902 --> 01:11:46,752
A fine-gauge, absorbable suture such as chromic gut or a delayedabsorbable type such as polyglactin 910 (Vicryl) or poliglecaprone (Monocryl) are suitable.

595
01:11:46,752 --> 01:11:50,877
If a site is hemostatic, skin glue is another option .

596
01:11:50,877 --> 01:11:55,664
Second-degree laceration is shown in Figures 27-11.

597
01:11:55,664 --> 01:12:00,577
Midline and mediolateral episiotomy repairs use similar steps.

598
01:12:00,577 --> 01:12:11,252
Namely, these close the vaginal epithelium and reapproximate the bulbospongiosus and superficial transverse perineal muscles during restoration of the perineal body.

599
01:12:11,252 --> 01:12:27,914
Compared with placing interrupted sutures, a continuous suturing method closes the defect faster, and has lower short-term pain scores, but wound complications and long-term pain rates are comparable and low (Kettle, 2012; Kindberg, 2008;

600
01:12:28,084 --> 01:12:31,059
Valenzuela, 2009).

601
01:12:31,009 --> 01:12:37,759
Selected less often, blunt needles are suitable and aim to decrease the incidence of needle-stick injuries .

602
01:12:37,759 --> 01:12:42,871
Commonly used suture materials are chromic catgut or 2–0 Vicryl.

603
01:12:42,871 --> 01:12:50,821
Compared with chromic catgut, Vicryl is associated with less shortterm discomfort, but long-term pain rates are comparable .

604
01:12:50,821 --> 01:12:58,234
However, traditional Vicryl occasionally requires removal of residual suture from the repair site because of pain.

605
01:12:58,234 --> 01:13:09,834
This disadvantage may be reduced by selecting a rapidly absorbed polyglactin 910 suture (Vicryl Rapide) (Greenberg, 2004; Kettle, 2002).

606
01:13:09,834 --> 01:13:16,021
Other delayed absorbable options include Monocryl or polydioxanone (PDS II).

607
01:13:16,021 --> 01:13:23,684
The perineal skin is closed by a running subcuticular stitch with the same suture type used to close the perineal defect.

608
01:13:23,684 --> 01:13:26,434
Figure 27-11.

609
01:13:26,434 --> 01:13:29,246
Midline episiotomy repair.

610
01:13:29,246 --> 01:13:41,259
A. An anchor stitch is placed above the wound apex to begin a running, locking closure with 2–0 suture to close the vaginal epithelium and deeper tissues and reapproximate the hymeneal ring.

611
01:13:41,259 --> 01:13:46,471
B. A transition stitch redirects suturing from the vagina to the perineum.

612
01:13:46,471 --> 01:13:56,984
C. The superficial transverse perineal and bulbospongiosus muscles are reapproximated using a continuous, nonlocking technique with the same length of suture.

613
01:13:56,984 --> 01:14:01,621
This aids restoration of the perineal body for long-term support.

614
01:14:01,621 --> 01:14:07,059
D. The continuous suture is then carried upward as a subcuticular stitch.

615
01:14:07,059 --> 01:14:11,009
The final knot is tied proximal to the hymeneal ring.

616
01:14:11,009 --> 01:14:18,584
Reproduced with permission from Kenton K, Mueller M: Episiotomy and obstetric anal sphincter lacerations.

617
01:14:18,584 --> 01:14:27,896
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

618
01:14:27,896 --> 01:14:32,384
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

619
01:14:32,384 --> 01:14:38,159
For third-degree laceration repair, two methods are available to repair the EAS.

620
01:14:38,159 --> 01:14:44,559
The first is an end-to-end technique, which we prefer, and is shown in Figure 27-12.

621
01:14:44,559 --> 01:14:50,234
Initially, the torn ends of the EAS, which often retract, are isolated.

622
01:14:50,234 --> 01:14:54,959
The EAS and its surrounding connective tissue are brought to the midline.

623
01:14:54,959 --> 01:14:59,546
As a misnomer, this connective tissue is often called a capsule .

624
01:14:59,546 --> 01:15:06,621
The strength of this closure is derived from the connective tissue surrounding the sphincter and less so from the striated muscle.

625
01:15:06,621 --> 01:15:16,409
Thus, serial interrupted sutures incorporate sphincter fibers and a substantial portion of the perisphincter connective tissue to bring EAS ends together.

626
01:15:16,409 --> 01:15:26,421
If the IAS is torn, a running, nonlocking closure is completed with 3-0 or 4-0 suture (see Fig. 27-13B).

627
01:15:26,421 --> 01:15:35,109
Few data guide suture selection for sphincter repair, but delayed-absorbable material can provide sustained tensile strength during healing .

628
01:15:35,109 --> 01:15:45,734
This theory is supported by the study by Jallad and coworkers (2016), which showed a higher perineal breakdown rate following OASIS repair with chromic catgut.

629
01:15:45,734 --> 01:15:48,409
Figure 27-12.

630
01:15:48,409 --> 01:16:08,821
In overview, with end-to-end approximation of the external anal sphincter (EAS), a suture is placed through the EAS muscle, and four to six simple interrupted 2–0 or 3–0 sutures of polyglactin 910 are placed at the 3, 6, 9, and 12 o’clock positions through the perisphincter connective tissue.

631
01:16:08,821 --> 01:16:15,296
To begin, disrupted ends of the striated EAS muscle and capsule are identified and grasped.

632
01:16:15,296 --> 01:16:19,971
The first suture is placed posteriorly to maintain clear exposure.

633
01:16:19,971 --> 01:16:24,671
Another suture is then placed inferiorly at the 6 o’clock position.

634
01:16:24,671 --> 01:16:29,534
The sphincter muscle fibers are next reapposed by a figure-of-eight stitch.

635
01:16:29,534 --> 01:16:38,196
Last, the remainder of the fascia is closed with a stitch placed anterior to the sphincter cylinder and again with one placed superior to it.

636
01:16:38,196 --> 01:16:45,771
Reproduced with permission from Kenton K, Mueller M: Episiotomy and obstetric anal sphincter lacerations.

637
01:16:45,771 --> 01:16:55,084
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

638
01:16:55,084 --> 01:16:59,571
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

639
01:16:59,571 --> 01:17:02,446
Figure 27-13.

640
01:17:02,446 --> 01:17:16,759
A. Suturing of the anorectal mucosa begins above the laceration apex using a continuous, nonlocking method with fine-gauge absorbable suture such as 3–0 or 4–0 chromic gut or polyglactin 910.

641
01:17:16,759 --> 01:17:24,709
Sutures are placed through the anorectal submucosa approximately 0.5 cm apart down to the anal verge.

642
01:17:24,709 --> 01:17:26,496
B. A

643
01:17:26,668 --> 01:17:33,905
second reinforcing layer uses 3–0 delayed-absorbable suture in a continuous, nonlocking fashion.

644
01:17:33,855 --> 01:17:47,255
This incorporates the torn ends of the internal anal sphincter (IAS), which can be identified as the glistening white fibrous structure lying between the anal canal submucosa and the fibers of the external anal sphincter.

645
01:17:47,255 --> 01:17:53,593
In many cases, the IAS retracts laterally and must be sought and retrieved for repair.

646
01:17:53,593 --> 01:18:01,168
Reproduced with permission from Kenton K, Mueller M: Episiotomy and obstetric anal sphincter lacerations.

647
01:18:01,168 --> 01:18:10,480
In Yeomans ER, Hoffman BL, Gilstrap LC III, et al (eds): Cunningham and Gilstrap’s Operative Obstetrics, 3rd ed.

648
01:18:10,480 --> 01:18:14,968
New York, NY: McGraw Hill; 2017.

649
01:18:14,968 --> 01:18:23,268
With the overlapping technique, the ends of the EAS are brought to the midline, lie atop one another, and are sutured in this position.

650
01:18:23,268 --> 01:18:29,680
This method is suitable only for type 3c lacerations—those with complete EAS rupture.

651
01:18:29,680 --> 01:18:36,180
Two rows of mattress sutures travel through both overlapping EAS ends to recreate the anal ring.

652
01:18:36,180 --> 01:18:48,730
In comparing the two methods, neither yields superior long-term anatomical or functional results (Farrell, 2012; Fernando, 2013; Fitzpatrick, 2000).

653
01:18:48,730 --> 01:18:55,930
Also with type 3c lacerations, the IAS is repaired before the EAS and is described next.

654
01:18:55,930 --> 01:19:05,168
With fourth-degree laceration repairs, both torn edges of the rectal mucosa are reapproximated first (see Fig. 27-13).

655
01:19:05,168 --> 01:19:17,280
Starting at a point 1 cm proximal to the wound apex, sutures are placed approximately 0.5 cm apart in the rectal muscularis and do not enter the anorectal lumen.

656
01:19:17,280 --> 01:19:23,980
Clinicians often use 4–0 delayed-absorbable suture or chromic gut for this running suture line.

657
01:19:23,980 --> 01:19:27,830
Some recommend a second reinforcing layer above this .

658
01:19:27,830 --> 01:19:33,968
The next layer to cover the anorectal mucosa is formed by reapproximation of the IAS.

659
01:19:33,968 --> 01:19:50,793
For reduction of infectious morbidity associated with OASIS, a single dose of antibiotic at the time of repair may be considered (Buppasiri, 2014; Duggal, 2008; Lewicky-Gaupp, 2015; Stock, 2013).

660
01:19:50,793 --> 01:19:57,868
A single dose of a second-generation cephalosporin is suitable, or clindamycin for penicillinallergic women.

661
01:19:57,868 --> 01:20:05,968
With OASIS, postoperatively, stool softeners are prescribed for a week, and enemas and suppositories are avoided.

662
01:20:05,968 --> 01:20:09,093
# Perineal Laceration Care.

663
01:20:09,093 --> 01:20:17,393
Initially, locally applied ice packs help reduce swelling and allay discomfort (de Souza Bosco Paiva, 2016).

664
01:20:17,393 --> 01:20:22,168
In subsequent days, warm sitz baths aid comfort and hygiene.

665
01:20:22,168 --> 01:20:28,268
Additionally, a small squirt bottle of warm water can cleanse the site after voiding or stooling.

666
01:20:28,268 --> 01:20:32,580
Analgesics containing codeine provide considerable relief.

667
01:20:32,580 --> 01:20:38,543
For lesser degrees of discomfort, nonsteroidal antiinflammatory drugs can be given.

668
01:20:38,543 --> 01:20:50,980
Because pain may instead signal a large vulvar, paravaginal, or ischiorectal fossa hematoma or perineal cellulitis, these sites should be examined carefully if pain is severe or persistent.

669
01:20:50,980 --> 01:20:59,955
Management of these complications is discussed in Chapters 37 and 42 (pp. 657 and 740).

670
01:20:59,955 --> 01:21:10,180
In addition to pain, urinary retention may complicate episiotomy recovery, and management is described in Chapter 36 (p. 643).

671
01:21:10,180 --> 01:21:18,105
For those with second-degree lacerations or OASIS, intercourse is usually proscribed until after the first puerperal visit.

672
01:21:18,105 --> 01:21:33,530
Compared with women with intact perineum, those with perineal trauma show higher rates of delayed intercourse at 3 and 6 months, but not at 1 year (McDonald, 2015; Rådestad, 2008; Signorello, 2001).

